    { marks: 5, q: 'What is communalism? State the different forms of communal politics with one example each.',
      keypoints: [
        { text: 'Definition: religion as principal basis of community; one religion superior', kw: ['religion', 'superior|basis|one community'] },
        { text: 'Everyday beliefs: stereotypes, prejudices', kw: ['stereotyp|prejudice|everyday'] },
        { text: 'Political dominance: majoritarianism or separate political unit', kw: ['dominan|majoritarian', 'separate'] },
        { text: 'Political mobilisation: sacred symbols, religious leaders, fear', kw: ['mobilis|mobiliz', 'symbol|leader|fear'] },
        { text: 'Communal violence: riots, massacre at Partition', kw: ['riot|violence', 'partition'] }
      ],
      model: 'Communalism is politics based on the belief that religion is the principal basis of social community — that one religion is superior and that followers of one religion form one community whose interests are opposed to those of others.\n1. Everyday beliefs: religious prejudices, stereotypes of communities and belief in the superiority of one\'s own religion. Example: thinking that people of another religion are all alike.\n2. Political dominance: for the majority community, majoritarian dominance; for the minority community, a desire to form a separate political unit.\n3. Political mobilisation on religious lines: using sacred symbols, religious leaders, emotional appeal and plain fear to bring one religious group together, often during elections.\n4. Communal violence: in its ugliest form, communalism leads to riots and massacre. India and Pakistan suffered some of the worst communal riots at the time of Partition.' },
    { marks: 5, q: 'How do gender divisions get expressed in politics? Explain the role of feminist movements and the need for women\'s political representation.',
      keypoints: [
        { text: 'Sexual division of labour confined women to private sphere', kw: ['division of labour|private'] },
        { text: 'Women agitated for voting rights, education, careers', kw: ['vot', 'educat|career'] },
        { text: 'Feminist movements: equality in personal and family life', kw: ['feminist', 'family|personal'] },
        { text: 'Women\'s role in public life improved; Sweden, Norway, Finland', kw: ['sweden|norway|finland'] },
        { text: 'Low representation in India; reservation in local bodies; 2023 law', kw: ['one-third|one third', 'reserv'] }
      ],
      model: '1. Because of the sexual division of labour, women were confined to the private sphere of the home, and public life, especially politics, was dominated by men.\n2. Women in different parts of the world organised and agitated for equal rights: voting rights, better educational and career opportunities and a better political and legal status.\n3. More radical women\'s movements, called feminist movements, aimed at equality in personal and family life as well.\n4. This political expression of gender issues improved women\'s role in public life. In Sweden, Norway and Finland, women\'s participation in public life is very high.\n5. In India women\'s representation is still low (about 14% in the Lok Sabha in 2019). Issues of women\'s welfare get attention only when women control power, so one-third of seats in panchayats and municipalities are reserved for women, and the Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam, 2023 reserves one-third of seats in the Lok Sabha and state assemblies.' },
    { marks: 5, q: 'Explain the different ways in which caste can take various forms in politics, and how politics influences caste.',
      keypoints: [
        { text: 'Candidates chosen by caste composition; parties appeal to caste sentiment', kw: ['candidate', 'sentiment|appeal'] },
        { text: 'Universal adult franchise made leaders mobilise caste groups', kw: ['franchise|one person'] },
        { text: 'Castes include neighbouring castes/sub-castes; form coalitions', kw: ['sub-caste|neighbour', 'coalition'] },
        { text: 'New groups: backward and forward', kw: ['backward', 'forward'] },
        { text: 'Positive: Dalits/OBCs gain power; negative: diverts attention, violence', kw: ['dalit|obc', 'divert|violence|tension'] }
      ],
      model: 'Caste in politics:\n1. When parties choose candidates, they keep in mind the caste composition of the electorate, and parties and candidates appeal to caste sentiment to muster support. Some parties are known to favour some castes.\n2. Universal adult franchise and the principle of one person, one vote compelled leaders to mobilise and organise caste groups, and gave new consciousness to castes earlier treated as inferior.\nPolitics in caste:\n3. Each caste group tries to become bigger by incorporating neighbouring castes or sub-castes, and enters into a coalition with other castes.\n4. New kinds of caste groups, like "backward" and "forward" castes, have come up in politics.\n5. Effects: positively, disadvantaged groups like Dalits and OBC castes gained better access to decision-making. Negatively, exclusive focus on caste diverts attention from poverty, development and corruption, and can lead to tension and violence.' },
    { marks: 5, q: 'Is it right to say that elections in India are all about caste? Explain with reasons.',
      keypoints: [
        { text: 'Caste matters: candidates and appeals based on caste', kw: ['candidate', 'caste'] },
        { text: 'No constituency has a single caste majority', kw: ['majority', 'constituenc'] },
        { text: 'No party gets all votes of a caste; voters have many loyalties', kw: ['loyalt|all the vote'] },
        { text: 'Ruling party and sitting MPs often lose', kw: ['lose|lost|defeat'] },
        { text: 'Economic issues, performance and leaders also matter', kw: ['econom|performance|poverty'] }
      ],
      model: 'No. Caste does play a role: parties choose candidates keeping in mind the caste composition of voters and appeal to caste sentiment. But elections are not all about caste:\n1. No parliamentary constituency in India has a clear majority of one single caste, so every candidate and party needs the support of more than one caste and community.\n2. No party wins the votes of all the voters of a caste. When people say a caste is a "vote bank" of one party, it usually means a large proportion of that caste votes for it — not all.\n3. Voters have many loyalties; many candidates may be from the same caste, so voters must choose on other grounds.\n4. The ruling party and sitting MPs frequently lose elections, which would not happen if caste decided everything.\n5. Voters also give importance to economic issues like poverty and prices, the performance of the government, and the popularity of leaders.' }
